{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q020_syntaxin_fragment_reclassification","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 makes the early structures partial-domain observations, not competing evidence that the syntaxin N-terminal domain has an autonomous spectrin-repeat fold. It classifies d1br0a_ and all three 1ez3 domains (d1ez3a_, d1ez3b_, d1ez3c_) together with full-domain d1dn1b_ in class a (all-alpha), STAT-like fold a.47, t-SNARE superfamily a.47.2, and t-SNARE family a.47.2.1. The shared family is the strongest relationship here and supports a close homologous relationship among these syntaxin domains; fold similarity alone would not.

Crucially, every 1br0/1ez3 domain is annotated “three-helical fragment; similar to one spectrin repeat” and has an ms caveat: secondary structures comprising less than one-third of the common domain are missing. The STAT-like fold annotation describes a four-long-helix bundle. Thus the spectrin-like appearance is best interpreted as the local appearance of an incomplete three-helix portion of the four-helix syntaxin domain. These fragments should not be counted as independent support for spectrin homology, convergence, or a separate fold, and absent positions must not be interpreted as biological deletions. In contrast, d1dn1b_ has the same a.47.2.1 placement without the fragment caveat; its current classification is an automated match to d3c98b_. The 1dn1 PDB entry itself is obsolete and replaced by 3c98, which matters for obtaining current coordinates but does not weaken the family assignment.

For template use, 1br0/1ez3 can represent only their covered three-helix core; they are not interchangeable with a complete-domain template and cannot define the missing helix, full-domain conformation, or interaction state. SCOPe does not identify a best template without complete-candidate quality records. A stronger claim connecting syntaxin to spectrin would require complete-domain structural alignment, core sequence correspondence, and phylogenetic or literature evidence. Transfer of detailed function, regulation, binding interactions, or conformational behavior additionally requires sequence coverage, biological-state and ligand/complex context, coordinate-quality assessment, and biochemical evidence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info — /api/v1/releases/current","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry — /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/domains; /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/domains; /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1; /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3c_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3c_/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3c_/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3c_/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/homology"